#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078231 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078566 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b08650da-89e7-5a9d-89a6-de3623344662" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating the Value of a Function at a Point Using a Tangent Line Approximation"}] :question/id #uuid "aea27069-47b4-50b0-9e70-3a65951916b8" :question/math-academy-id "e-291" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(-2) = 0$ and $f'(-2)=2.$ Estimate the value of $f(-1.8)$ using the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x=-2.$" :question/worked-solution "The tangent line approximation to $f(x)$ at the point $a=-2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =f(-2)+{f}^{′}(-2)(x-(-2)) \\\\  & =0+2(x+2) \\\\  & =2(x+2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $f(-1.8)$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=-1.8.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1.8) & ≈L(-1.8) \\\\  & =2(-1.8+2) \\\\  & =2(0.2) \\\\  & =0.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078232 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078567 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b0dd714-5bf1-573f-9ff9-11802622ce92" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating the Value of a Function at a Point Given its Derivative"}] :question/id #uuid "44510fee-857c-5182-8f9e-a08c18966142" :question/math-academy-id "e-292" :question/problem "Let $g$ be a function such that $g'(x) =\\sqrt{x}$ and $g(4) = 3.$ Using the tangent line approximation of $g(x)$ at $x=4,$ estimate the value of $g(3.6).$" :question/worked-solution "The tangent line approximation to $g(x)$ at the point $a=4$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =g(a)+{g}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =g(4)+{g}^{′}(4)(x-4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $g(4)=3,$ and since $g'(x)=\\sqrt{x},$ then $g'(4)=\\sqrt{4}=2.$ So, the tangent line approximation is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =3+2(x-4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can approximate $g(3.6)$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=3.6.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(3.6) & ≈L(3.6) \\\\  & =3+2(3.6-4) \\\\  & =3+2(-0.4) \\\\  & =3-0.8 \\\\  & =2.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078236 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078568 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9f542453-9758-55e6-93c6-6bfd2139ec45" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Tangent Line Approximation and Classifying it as an Underestimate or Overestimate"}] :question/id #uuid "dc311e80-fd79-5b89-9f0e-0b1caef6457e" :question/math-academy-id "e-293" :question/problem "The graph of the function $y=f(x)$ passes through $(-2,-5)$ and is concave down on the interval $(-\\infty,0).$ If $f'(-2)=3$, estimate the value of $f(-2.3)$ using the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x=-2$ and determine whether the approximation is an underestimate or overestimate." :question/worked-solution "The tangent line approximation to $f(x)$ at the point $a=-2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =f(-2)+{f}^{′}(-2)(x-(-2)) \\\\  & =f(-2)+{f}^{′}(-2)(x+2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function passes through the point $(-2,-5),$ so we have $f(-2)=-5.$ Also, we're told that $f'(-2)=3,$ so we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =-5+3(x+2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $f(-2.3)$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=-2.3.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-2.3) & ≈L(-2.3) \\\\  & =-5+3(-2.3+2) \\\\  & =-5+3(-0.3) \\\\  & =-5-0.9 \\\\  & =-5.9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the function is concave down on the interval $(-\\infty,0)$, the linear approximation $L(x)$ is above the graph near $x=-2,$ and therefore we have an overestimate."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297412 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078566 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b08650da-89e7-5a9d-89a6-de3623344662" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating the Value of a Function at a Point Using a Tangent Line Approximation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e3d35ca-ccf6-5b18-8a5e-78467bdfc92c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295350}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0e3d35ca-ccf6-5b18-8a5e-78467bdfc92c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295350} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a43d106-9839-5bde-9a42-b20c59c9fdb4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296917}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ce7b136c-2ab3-5bfd-98fd-46fda48c1806" :question/math-academy-id "q-314895" :question/problem "An environmental researcher is tracking the depth $d$, in meters, of a lake during a dry season. The following table shows the rate at which the depth is decreasing.\n\n| **$t\\,\\text{(weeks)}$** | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${d}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{(meters/week)}$ | $-0.3$ | $-0.5$ | $-0.8$ | $-1.0$ |\n\nAt $t=2,$ the lake depth was $12$ meters. Use the tangent line approximation of $d$ at time $t=2$ to estimate the depth of the lake at $t=2.4.$ Do not round the answer.\n\nThe depth of the lake at $t=2.4$ weeks is estimated to be\n{{field-1}}\nmeters." :question/worked-solution "The tangent line approximation of $d(t)$ at the point $a=2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(t) & =d(a)+{d}^{′}(a)(t-a) \\\\  & =d(2)+{d}^{′}(2)(t-2) \\\\  & =12+(-0.5)(t-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $d(2.4)$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $t=2.4.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d(2.4) & ≈L(2.4) \\\\  & =12+(-0.5)(2.4-2) \\\\  & =12+(-0.5)(0.4) \\\\  & =12-0.2 \\\\  & =11.8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe depth of the lake at $t=2.4$ weeks is estimated to be $11.8$ meters."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319052 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078566 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b08650da-89e7-5a9d-89a6-de3623344662" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating the Value of a Function at a Point Using a Tangent Line Approximation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4466b5c4-7137-58ba-bc9b-618a5d1ada38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317787}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4466b5c4-7137-58ba-bc9b-618a5d1ada38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317787} :answer-field/id #uuid "e78dd783-f2e3-59e1-b7f8-93cab4470141" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318911}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e03e29d0-50e9-528b-9c71-b52ed36384c2" :question/math-academy-id "q-346041" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(-4)=1$ and ${f}^{′}(-4)=6.$ Using the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x=-4,$ estimate the value of $f(-3.8).$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$f(-3.8)≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at the point $a=-4$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =f(-4)+{f}^{′}(-4)(x-(-4)) \\\\  & =1+6(x+4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $f(-3.8)$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=-3.8.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-3.8) & ≈L(-3.8) \\\\  & =1+6(-3.8+4) \\\\  & =1+6(0.2) \\\\  & =1+1.2 \\\\  & =2.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078972 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078567 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b0dd714-5bf1-573f-9ff9-11802622ce92" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating the Value of a Function at a Point Given its Derivative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "93ba420b-c3fd-5a50-8e9b-11513b6a2669" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076611} {:answer/id #uuid "b62547eb-8707-55b0-94a4-a6d5df4cc174" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076612} {:answer/id #uuid "d3bb45a6-9566-5634-9213-c1a0fad92def" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076613} {:answer/id #uuid "301497a6-d0ac-5fbd-b4d7-625a04c53aee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076614} {:answer/id #uuid "3cc5fe14-3cdf-51bc-8db0-fa04ac2eba86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076615}] :answer-field/id #uuid "0c1483f4-1168-5fb2-b5ec-51249976c4aa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076610}] :question/id #uuid "70b30b18-9330-5710-9320-15027fb2107e" :question/math-academy-id "q-4802" :question/problem "Let $f$ be a function such that $f^{′}(x) = 5x - 3$ and $f(2) =-1$. Use the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x = 2$ to estimate the value of $f(2.4)$.\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329871 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078567 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b0dd714-5bf1-573f-9ff9-11802622ce92" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating the Value of a Function at a Point Given its Derivative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "185bd225-27ea-5674-95ca-a56c74418c0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8.75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327144} {:answer/id #uuid "48bd7b8a-015d-50ab-bb38-609edbfcaab3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327567} {:answer/id #uuid "81aeeb53-2a67-5879-907e-d250a9dcc6ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328052} {:answer/id #uuid "8ccede01-5805-5b15-954a-9a60f2ec5889" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328145} {:answer/id #uuid "c3ef5572-1d9c-55ef-acf9-cb700da795f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328651}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "48bd7b8a-015d-50ab-bb38-609edbfcaab3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327567} :answer-field/id #uuid "a6a72543-d7b3-5d16-9023-ad6d4845370a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329471}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e4ab790a-069c-5f5b-8f9d-fdcc980a31a3" :question/math-academy-id "q-4813" :question/problem "Let $f$ be a function such that ${f}^{′}(x)=2x$ and $f(2)=4.$ Use the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x=2$ to estimate the value of $f(3).$" :question/worked-solution "The tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at the point $a=2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =f(2)+{f}^{′}(2)(x-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $f(2)=4,$ and since ${f}^{′}(x)=2x,$ then ${f}^{′}(2)=2(2)=4.$ So, the tangent line approximation is given by\n\n$$\nL(x)=4+4(x-2).\n$$\n\nNow, we can approximate $f(3)$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=3.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(3) & ≈L(3) \\\\  & =4+4(3-2) \\\\  & =4+4(1) \\\\  & =4+4 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078997 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078568 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9f542453-9758-55e6-93c6-6bfd2139ec45" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Tangent Line Approximation and Classifying it as an Underestimate or Overestimate"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b0860b2b-aabc-53ae-9a72-abeec6df32ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(-1.8) \\approx 1.8$, an overestimate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076761} {:answer/id #uuid "d35913b2-83ac-5fa6-8cf6-d318005c96a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(-1.8) \\approx 1.8$, an underestimate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076762} {:answer/id #uuid "e33fd5b6-4bd2-595a-951b-700dc34c630f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(-1.8) \\approx 1.4$, an underestimate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076763} {:answer/id #uuid "e9c70643-4f2e-52eb-b209-4d49c918415a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(-1.8) \\approx 1.5$, an underestimate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076764} {:answer/id #uuid "7691ad3f-d40f-578d-b11a-f63ab8084d83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(-1.8) \\approx 1.4$, an overestimate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076765}] :answer-field/id #uuid "f1ab2aaa-c802-5a4a-8b0c-21677aabcdc1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076760}] :question/id #uuid "ab2f6e79-7a14-5396-9023-6cf5ec22b244" :question/math-academy-id "q-48402" :question/problem "The graph of the function $y = f(x)$ passes through $(-2, 2)$ and is concave up on the interval $(-3,-1)$. If $f^{′}(-2) =-1$, estimate the value of $f(-1.8)$ using the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x =-2$ and determine whether the approximation is an underestimate or overestimate.\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402957 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078568 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9f542453-9758-55e6-93c6-6bfd2139ec45" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Tangent Line Approximation and Classifying it as an Underestimate or Overestimate"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5cf36fa0-f53d-5a7d-b768-a8a6d96fa3d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(3.2)≈-2.2,$ an overestimate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402959} {:answer/id #uuid "c863ec26-7875-52c6-8e8e-ef3828506796" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(3.2)≈-2.6,$ an overestimate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402960} {:answer/id #uuid "a196f4d2-df4e-518f-b800-04deefc67cdc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(3.2)≈-2.6,$ an underestimate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402961} {:answer/id #uuid "0856fb24-85cf-5798-a1f2-7784442e16d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(3.2)≈-2.2,$ an underestimate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402962} {:answer/id #uuid "5fdd5bf8-16a7-5589-8bf3-a8302c740846" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(3.2)≈-3.4,$ an overestimate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402963}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5cf36fa0-f53d-5a7d-b768-a8a6d96fa3d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(3.2)≈-2.2,$ an overestimate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402959} :answer-field/id #uuid "b7c924a1-67fd-5e0c-8035-7de5293c5ea0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402958}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "23fa4cab-6951-5a21-810d-7733098a8e5c" :question/math-academy-id "q-48404" :question/problem "The graph of the function $y=f(x)$ passes through $(3,-3)$ and is concave down on the interval $(1,4).$ If ${f}^{′}(3)=4,$ estimate the value of $f(3.2)$ using the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x=3$ and determine whether the approximation is an underestimate or overestimate." :question/worked-solution "The tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at the point $a=3$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =f(3)+{f}^{′}(3)(x-3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function passes through the point $(3,-3),$ so we have $f(3)=-3.$ Also, we're told that ${f}^{′}(3)=4,$ so we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =-3+4(x-3) \\\\  & =-3+4x-12 \\\\  & =4x-15.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $f(3.2)$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=3.2.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(3.2) & ≈L(3.2) \\\\  & =4(3.2)-15 \\\\  & =12.8-15 \\\\  & =-2.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the function is concave down on the interval $(1,4),$ the linear approximation $L(x)$ is above the graph near $x=3,$ and therefore we have an overestimate."}]}
